.TH RM 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
rm \- remove files and directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rm
.RB [ \-fiRrv ]
.IR file ...\&
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rm
removes each
.IR file ,
and with
.B \-r
each directory and everything in it.
It is the
.BR busybox (1)
applet, which has fewer options than GNU
.BR rm ;
see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
Removal is permanent.
Nothing goes to the Recycle Bin; for that, use
.BR recycle (1).
.SS Operands
Without
.BR \-r ,
a directory operand is an error.
With
.BR \-r ,
a directory is emptied recursively and then removed.
A symbolic link, junction, or app execution alias is removed itself,
with or without
.BR \-r ,
and never followed, even with a trailing slash.
Such a link found inside a directory is removed the same way.
.PP
Trailing slashes and backslashes are removed from each operand first,
down to a root such as
.BR c:/ .
An operand whose last component is
.B .\&
or
.BR .. ,
or that is only a drive, such as
.BR c: ,
is refused, even with
.BR \-f ,
and the other operands are still processed; see
.BR NOTES .
No other operand is protected:
.B rm \-r c:/
removes everything it can from the root of the drive.
.PP
With no operand,
.B rm
writes a usage message on standard error and exits with status 1.
With no operand and
.BR \-f ,
it does nothing and exits with status 0.
.SS Order and failures
.B rm
handles the operands in order.
It reports a failure, goes on with the remaining operands, and exits
with status 1 at the end.
The entries of a directory are removed in the order Windows lists them,
each before the directory itself.
If any entry could not be removed, the directory is left alone with no
further message, though
.B \-i
still asks about it and
.B \-v
still reports it removed.
A directory that cannot be opened for reading is left alone with status
1 and no message.
.SS Prompting
With
.BR \-i ,
.B rm
asks before removing each file, and for each directory it asks before
entering it and again before removing it.
Without
.BR \-f ,
it also asks before removing a read-only file, but only when standard
input is a console.
It does not ask about a link this way.
Windows reports every directory as writable, even one with the read-only
attribute, so a directory is asked about only with
.BR \-i .
.PP
The questions are written on standard error, with no newline after the
question mark:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
rm: remove \(aqfile\(aq?
rm: descend into directory \(aqdir\(aq?
rm: remove directory \(aqdir\(aq?
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B rm
then reads a line from standard input.
The answer is yes if the first character of the line that is not a
space or tab is
.B y
or
.BR Y .
Anything else, including the end of input, is no, which skips the item
and is not an error.
A no to the question about descending leaves the directory and its
contents alone.
A no for a file in a directory followed by a yes for the directory
leaves the directory not empty, so its removal fails with
.BR "Directory not empty" ,
and the exit status is 1.
.SS Output
.B rm
writes each error on standard error, preceded by its name and a colon.
The messages are:
.TP
.BI "can\(aqt remove \(aq" path "\(aq: " reason
The path does not exist and
.B \-f
was not given, or Windows would not remove it.
.TP
.BI "can\(aqt stat \(aq" path "\(aq: " reason
The path could not be examined, for a reason other than that it does not
exist.
.TP
.BI \(aq dir "\(aq is a directory"
A directory operand without
.BR \-r .
.TP
.B "can\(aqt remove \(aq.\(aq or \(aq..\(aq"
An operand was refused.
.PP
Only
.B \-v
and
.B \-\-help
write to standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-rf ,
and may follow operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Apart from
.BR \-\-help ,
there are no long options.
.TP
.B \-i
Ask before every removal; see
.BR Prompting .
Of
.B \-f
and
.BR \-i ,
only the last given takes effect.
.TP
.B \-f
Never ask.
Ignore an operand that does not exist, with no message and no error.
Every other error is still reported, with status 1, such as a directory
without
.BR \-r ,
an operand of
.BR .\& ,
.BR .. ,
or only a drive, and a failed removal.
Of
.B \-f
and
.BR \-i ,
only the last given takes effect.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-r
Remove directories and their contents recursively.
.TP
.B \-v
Write
.BI removed\ \(aq file \(aq
on standard output after each file or link is removed, and
.BI removed\ directory:\ \(aq dir \(aq
after each directory, which comes after its contents.
Nothing is written for an item that is skipped or fails, but a
directory left in place because an entry in it could not be removed is
still reported as removed.
The usage message omits this option.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument only, write a usage message on standard output
and exit with status 0.
The message omits
.BR \-v .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when busybox starts, for a caller
such as
.B cmd.exe
that does not expand them.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for the programs it starts, so commands run from the kit's shell get the
shell's expansion only.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Startup and messages written to a console also depend on
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
as for every busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including
.B \-\-help
as the first argument, operands skipped by an answer to a question,
operands that do not exist with
.BR \-f ,
and no operands with
.BR \-f .
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line, with a usage message on standard error, or any
operand or directory entry that could not be removed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Remove the object files:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ rm *.o
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove a build directory and everything in it, without asking:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ rm \-rf build
.EE
.RE
.PP
Ask before removing each backup file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ rm \-i *.bak
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove a tree that holds a/b/f and g, and list each removal:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ rm \-rv v
removed \(aqv/a/b/f\(aq
removed directory: \(aqv/a/b\(aq
removed directory: \(aqv/a\(aq
removed \(aqv/g\(aq
removed directory: \(aqv\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove a file whose name begins with a dash:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ rm \-\- \-x
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove a junction or symbolic link to a directory, which needs no
.BR \-r ,
leaving the directory it points to alone:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ rm link
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR rm :
.IP \(bu 2
Only
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-r ,
and
.B \-v
exist.
Every other option is invalid, including
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-I ,
and every long option but
.BR \-\-help .
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BR \-\-preserve\-root .
Only an operand ending in
.B .\&
or
.BR .. ,
or one that is only a drive, is refused.
.PP
.B rm
clears the read-only attribute of a file before it removes it, and of a
directory once the directory is empty, so read-only items are removed.
If the removal then fails, the attribute stays cleared.
Hidden and system files are removed like any other.
.PP
Windows does not remove a file that another program has open without
allowing deletion, or a directory that is the current directory of a
running program, such as a shell.
The removal fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
With
.BR \-r ,
the rest of the tree is still removed, apart from the directories that
hold the item.
.PP
A full path of 260 or more characters, counting the current directory
for a relative path, cannot be reached.
Without
.BR \-f ,
.B rm
reports
.B "No such file or directory"
for it.
With
.BR \-f ,
it skips it silently, and the directory that holds it then fails with
.BR "Directory not empty" .
A directory whose full path has 258 or more characters cannot be listed,
and is left alone with status 1 and no message.
Either way the directories above it are left too, and
.B rm
goes on.
.PP
An operand may have a drive letter and backslashes.
The names found in a directory are joined to the operand with
.BR / ,
so
.B rm \-rv \(aqc:\etmp\ex\(aq
writes names such as
.IR c:\etmp\ex/y .
Names may contain any Unicode character on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
An operand that is only a drive, such as
.BR c: ,
names the current directory of that drive, as
.B c:.\&
does, and is refused in the same way.
.PP
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
or any caller that leaves
.B BB_GLOBBING
unset,
.B rm
expands wildcards in its operands itself, even quoted ones.
A pattern that matches nothing is left as it is, so
.B rm
reports
.B "No such file or directory"
for it, or with
.B \-f
quietly succeeds.
To delete the files that a search selects, see
.B find \-delete
in
.BR find (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR recycle (1),
.BR rmdir (1)
